{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a merge game whose board has become gridlocked because too many items must fit on a small board. The later archiving feature does not solve the capacity problem because archived items still consume inventory space, leaving the player with insufficient storage for continued play. This is a realized inventory-capacity shortfall and supports Waste Aversion. The mention of insufficient energy describes a limited resource but does not state a recharge timer or waiting schedule, so it does not support Playing by Appointment. The complaint that the game differs from its ad concerns out-of-game advertising and is excluded under R13. The references to merging, unlocking items, and gems do not by themselves establish grinding, collection compulsion, or real-money currency conversion.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "game is now gridlocked. Way too many new things on a tiny board. Ridiculous. Update: they finally started \"archiving\" but expect you to use your inventory space for them???",
      "rule_applied": "definition; realized inventory-capacity shortfall",
      "rationale": "The board is blocked by too many items, and the supposed archiving solution still consumes limited inventory space, creating a concrete storage shortfall."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement mechanic: an ad is forced after every over, requiring the player to encounter it during normal cricket gameplay. The app closing when the ad is accidentally tapped is attributed to bad app behavior and is a technical malfunction, so it does not establish a separate dark-pattern label. The request to fix the UI is hypothetical and does not describe a confirmed UI-misdirection mechanism such as a hidden or undersized cancel control.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Ads are automatically shown at a fixed in-game event, compelling the player to view them during play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes in-game advertisements and pop-ups appearing continuously and sequentially before gameplay, delaying access to the game until the player has endured them. This is a forced-ad exposure mechanic. The review does not specify that the pop-ups are purchase prompts, that closing them is obstructed, or that they offer rewards for watching, so no additional label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "the seemingly endless in-game ads and pop-ups that cascade one after another so much that by the time I can actually play",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is effectively compelled to view repeated in-game ads before being able to play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes advertisements appearing inside the game despite the game being promoted as ad free. The player is required to interact with the advertisement and enter the app store to exit it, so the ad is not merely mentioned but functions as a forced interruption. The exit path is also obstructed because closing the ad requires going somewhere else and consumes additional data. This supports Advertisement and UI Misdirection. The claim that the game was advertised as ad free concerns external marketing and is not coded under R13.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to interact with an in-game advertisement and cannot simply continue without going through the play store."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "to get out of the ad, you have to go into the play store",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The ad obstructs the player's opt-out by routing the exit through the play store rather than providing a straightforward close control."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a streak-based daily return mechanic: the game tracks a streak, and when it says the player broke that streak, it resets their progress to the beginning and removes accumulated progress. Although the reviewer says this happened incorrectly, the underlying rule is still a daily reward or streak system that penalizes missing a day, so the bug attribution does not suppress the label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "said I broke my streak and though I did not, it sent me right back to the beginning",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11 exception for a malfunction triggering an underlying streak-reset penalty",
      "rationale": "A streak that resets progress when the game records a missed day is a daily-return incentive with an active penalty, even though the reviewer says the reset was erroneous."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes excessively long loading, which is a technical or service delay rather than a defined in-game timer or other coded mechanic. It also mentions a bonus feature that rarely appears or matches, but the meaning of “bonus lady” and “ww4” cannot be established from the review or the search results, so no reward-delivery label is assigned. The reviewer explicitly says they are buying coins from the game, describing real money being converted into in-game currency.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "buying coins from them",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes purchasing in-game coins with real money, which is the currency-acquisition pattern."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Cashman Casino \"WW4\" \"bonus lady\"",
  "search_result": "The search did not establish what “bonus lady” or “ww4” refers to in Cashman Casino, so those terms were not used to assign an additional label."
}

{
  "analysis": "The review reports several complaints but only one clearly describes a coded mechanic. The statement about cheating describes other players' conduct and does not say the game rewards or incentivizes cheating. Peg-E and the race car game are named events, but no specific event mechanic is described. Gold duplicates indicate unwanted sticker copies, but the review does not describe the randomized delivery process or a compulsion to complete the collection. The complaint about limited daily trades does describe an in-game exchange of stickers with other players; a search confirms that MONOPOLY GO! treats duplicate stickers as tradable with friends and imposes a daily trade limit. That supports Reciprocity. The lack of dice is resource scarcity alone and does not establish a listed label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Not enough trades a day!",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes a capped mechanic for trading resources with other players, which is an exchange-based favor mechanism."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "MONOPOLY GO gold duplicates trades a day sticker packs",
  "search_result": "The search established that duplicate stickers in MONOPOLY GO! can be traded with friends and that the game imposes a daily trade limit, supporting the interpretation of 'trades' as player-to-player resource exchange. ([monopolygo.helpshift.com](https://monopolygo.helpshift.com/hc/en/3-monopoly-go/faq/276-duplicate-stickers/?pubDate=20250530&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a competitive Ludo game in which the player plays against opponents. It also alleges that outcomes are deliberately manipulated: the player is supposedly meant to lose regardless of skill, with opponents receiving favorable dice counts while the reviewer receives unfavorable small counts. This is a claim that the game determines or tunes outcomes unfairly, which fits Illusion of Control. The separate mention of glitches is a technical complaint and does not itself create a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the player competing against opponents in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "if you are meant to lose, no matter how good you play you will definitley lose and in most of the cases this is the scene always..the opponents get good counts and you get small counts",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game fixes or manipulates dice outcomes so the player loses regardless of skill and opponents receive better counts."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Gardenscapes as an in-game match-three puzzle structure rather than the escape gameplay shown in advertising. The misleading advertisement is outside the app-design scope under R13, so it does not receive a label. Within the game, Austin's progress is tied to repeatedly playing the puzzle game to earn stars, and each game yields only one star; this names a repetitive activity required for character progress, supporting Grinding. The loss of points after failing a level is a failure penalty, but it does not describe a retention mechanic based on accumulated investment or a real-world time gate.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Even for walking Austin you will have to earn a star by playing stupid game.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Slow Progress; the span identifies the repeated game activity and ties it to progress",
      "rationale": "The player must repeatedly play the puzzle game to earn stars and advance Austin, which is a tedious task cycle required for progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes in-game advertisements that cannot be dismissed, so the player is forced to remain blocked by the ads and cannot continue playing normally. The absence of a usable close control obstructs the opt-out and requires restarting the game, which is a UI-level obstruction. This is not Mandatory Marathon because no lost progress or forfeited session is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Can't close the ads, therefore can't continue playing",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to deal with in-game advertisements because they cannot be closed before continuing play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Can't close the ads",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out",
      "rationale": "The advertisement lacks a usable dismissal path, obstructing the player's ability to opt out and continue."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an in-game purchase of a large pack of coins and says the purchase was accidental, followed by a refund. The accidental completion of the purchase indicates a low-friction purchase flow with insufficient confirmation, supporting Easy to Purchase. Because the purchased object is an in-game currency pack, the review also describes conversion of real-world money into virtual currency, supporting Intermediate Currency. The game's name includes casino slots, but the review does not describe a chance-based wager or reward mechanic, so Gambling is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "accidentally bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases",
      "rationale": "The reviewer reports that an in-game purchase was completed accidentally, implying the purchase flow lacked sufficient confirmation or undo protection."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "bought a huge coin pack",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency",
      "rationale": "Coins are the in-game currency being acquired through a purchase, indicating real money was exchanged for virtual currency."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes an in-game rewarded-ad mechanic: players watch advertisements with the expectation of receiving rewards. The reward delivery is then said to fail because the game reports that it \"can't connect,\" which is a technical fault; that failure itself is not coded as a dark pattern. The underlying incentive to watch ads for rewards is still explicitly present, so Advertisement applies. The generic mention of a microtransaction strategy does not identify a specific monetary mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "when you watch the ads, it conveniently \"can't connect\" so you can't get rewards for the ads you watched",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes advertisements watched in exchange for in-game rewards; the connection failure affects reward delivery but does not remove the underlying incentivized-ad mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an idle base-building game with heroes, scouting other bases, and gathering resources. The clearest dark-pattern mechanic is the use of building timers that make the player wait before construction progresses, which is a Wait to Play instance of Playing by Appointment. The request for more mini games or periodic rewards is hypothetical and does not establish an existing mechanic. Gathering resources is not described as repetitive or tied to a specific required progress cycle, and scouting other bases does not by itself establish competition.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "waiting for building times",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The game imposes building timers that require the player to wait for construction progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a level-entry resource system. Each level consumes credits, and the increasing cost leaves the player unable to continue for an hour unless they pay cash for more credits. The hour-long wait is an in-game timer that schedules when play can resume, supporting Playing by Appointment. Paying cash to obtain credits bypasses that access barrier, supporting Pay to Progress and Intermediate Currency because real money is converted into in-game credits. The example of needing 2.8k per level describes escalating cost, but does not by itself establish another coded mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "levels cost too much to play, which leaves you waiting an hour to play again",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "The game imposes a one-hour timer before the player can resume playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "or shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Skip; spend money to avoid waiting for a timer to expire",
      "rationale": "The player can pay for credits to bypass the barrier that otherwise prevents continued level play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "shell out cash for credits",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency",
      "rationale": "The span describes exchanging real-world cash for in-game credits used to play levels."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a 7-day streak that is lost when the game registers a problem, and they report this has happened three times. A streak is a daily-return reward mechanic with a penalty for losing accumulated progress, so this supports Daily Rewards. Although the reviewer says it happened for no reason and treats it as an issue, R11 allows the label because the underlying streak-reset rule is still the relevant game design behavior.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I've had 3 separate times times to where I've lost my 7 day streak for no reason whatsoever",
      "rule_applied": "definition; R11 bug attribution does not suppress the underlying streak-reset penalty",
      "rationale": "A 7-day streak that can be lost is a daily-return incentive with an explicit penalty for losing accumulated progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily mission system that limits what can be completed within the available attempts, implying a recurring daily task structure. It also describes an in-game timer that takes nearly two minutes to restore new chances to play, which imposes a wait before gameplay can continue. The complaint that items do not appear according to orders describes an unfavorable or possibly malfunctioning outcome, but it does not clearly establish a coded dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "The daily missions are impossible to do in such less attempts",
      "rule_applied": "Daily task lists with resetting rewards",
      "rationale": "The span identifies a daily mission system that constrains the player's tasks within a recurring daily cycle."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it takes nearly 2 minutes for new chances to play",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "New chances are restored only after an in-game timer, forcing the player to wait before playing again."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a casino slot game that is intentionally engaging but offers extremely poor chances of winning. The reviewer explicitly refers to returning to spend more money, so real money is being used in a luck-based game, which supports Gambling. However, the statement that winning is impossible is only a general complaint about outcomes; it does not describe rigging, near-misses, a fixed schedule, or a specific reward-delivery mechanism. It therefore does not support Illusion of Control, Optimism and Frequency Biases, or Reward Mania.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "why would I ever come back and spend more money when it is impossible to when",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes spending more money in a casino slots app, linking real-money participation to a luck-based chance game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes in-game advertisements that interrupt play and cannot be exited once they appear, so the player is compelled to watch them. The later inability to continue after the ad is attributed to the ad getting stuck, which is a technical malfunction and does not independently support a dark-pattern label under R11. The positive evaluation of the game is irrelevant to coding.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "cant back out of ads or continue once an ad comes on",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player cannot exit the in-game advertisement and is compelled to encounter it before continuing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}
